# Copy this file to deploy.env and fill in real values before running
# install-caddy.sh or radicale/install-radicale.sh. deploy.env itself is
# gitignored (see deploy/.gitignore) -- it holds a real domain and, once
# radicale/install-radicale.sh has run, nothing secret (the Radicale
# password is set interactively, never written to a file in this repo),
# but there's no reason to commit real domain names either.
#
# Order of operations (see deploy/README.md for the full walkthrough):
#   1. Buy/point a domain: A/AAAA records for DOMAIN_APP and DOMAIN_DAV,
#      pointing at this server's public IP. Wait for DNS to actually
#      resolve before step 3 -- Caddy's automatic HTTPS needs it.
#   2. cp deploy.env.example deploy.env, edit the values below.
#   3. sudo ./firewall.sh
#   4. sudo ./install-caddy.sh
#   5. sudo ./radicale/install-radicale.sh
#   6. Fill in CC_RADICALE_URL/USER/PASSWORD in /srv/curodav/shared/.env,
#      restart curodav.

# Public domain for the webapp itself. curodav.service already listens on
# 127.0.0.1:8000 (scripts/curodav-ctl's ExecStart) -- Caddy terminates TLS
# here and reverse-proxies to it.
DOMAIN_APP=app.example.com

# Public domain for Radicale (CalDAV/CardDAV) -- what DAVx5 points at.
# Keep this a SEPARATE subdomain from DOMAIN_APP, not a path under it --
# Radicale's own URL layout (/<user>/<collection>/) would otherwise collide
# with the webapp's own routes if they shared one hostname.
DOMAIN_DAV=dav.example.com

# Email Let's Encrypt uses for renewal/expiry notices -- required by
# Caddy's automatic HTTPS, never shown publicly.
LE_EMAIL=you@example.com

# The single Radicale user this app's own CC_RADICALE_USER authenticates
# as, and the same account DAVx5 logs into on the phone -- this is a
# single-user app, one account, not per-viewer accounts. Whatever you set
# here, use the SAME value for CC_RADICALE_USER in
# /srv/curodav/shared/.env afterward.
RADICALE_USER=curodav
